/* Section 33.2 clinical case cards: single best answer. Source: Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry, 12th ed., Chapter 33 (Neural Development and Neurogenesis). */
KS.add("ch33-2", "cases", [
  {"id":"c332-ov01","tag":"Neurulation","q":"A 26-year-old woman with epilepsy who is planning pregnancy asks which of her medications most raises the risk of her baby's spinal cord failing to close. She takes valproic acid. Which developmental process is at risk?","choices":["Neurulation in gestational weeks 3 to 4","Neuronal apoptosis in the cortex","Myelination in the second year of life","Gastrulation on gestational day 13","Synaptic pruning in adolescence"],"answer":0,"why":"Valproic acid is a well-known cause of neurulation defects; neural tube closure happens in weeks 3 to 4, and tail-end failure gives spina bifida (s332-over)."},
  {"id":"c332-ov02","tag":"Neurulation","q":"A prenatal ultrasound shows a fetus with absent forebrain and skull vault, consistent with failed closure at the head end of the neural tube. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lissencephaly","Tuberous sclerosis","Spina bifida","Neurofibromatosis","Anencephaly"],"answer":4,"why":"Failure of neurulation rostrally gives anencephaly; caudally it gives spina bifida (s332-over)."},
  {"id":"c332-ov03","tag":"Neurulation","q":"A 19-year-old woman using a retinoic acid skin preparation for acne learns she is pregnant. Her physician counsels her about a specific developmental risk. Which risk is most relevant?","choices":["Delayed puberty","Excess adult neurogenesis","Absent white matter in old age","Neural tube (neurulation) defect","Enlarged roof plate"],"answer":3,"why":"Retinoic acid in dermatologic preparations is a known cause of neurulation defects (s332-over)."},
  {"id":"c332-ov04","tag":"Neurulation","q":"A medical student reviews historical cases of children exposed to thalidomide in utero. Autism occurred only in those exposed within a narrow window. Which developmental principle does this best illustrate?","choices":["Growth factors act at many stages","Glia outnumber neurons","The neural plate is columnar epithelium","Neural crest cells migrate widely","Regions are generated on specific time schedules"],"answer":4,"why":"Different regions are generated at distinct times, so insults matter by timing; thalidomide caused autism only with exposure on days 20 to 24 of gestation (s332-over)."},
  {"id":"c332-ov05","tag":"Neurulation","q":"A 14-year-old boy has skin pigment lesions and seizures and is diagnosed with tuberous sclerosis. His mother asks why skin and brain are both involved. Which embryonic tissue best explains this pairing?","choices":["Neural crest","Floor plate","Primitive streak","Endoderm","Notochord"],"answer":0,"why":"The neural crest forms where neural ectoderm meets epidermal ectoderm and makes melanocytes, the basis of the neurocutaneous disorders (s332-over)."},
  {"id":"c332-ov06","tag":"Neurulation","q":"A research fellow finds that a patient group has reduced white matter on MRI but no obvious neuronal abnormality. A mentor says a primary neuron deficit could still be present. What best supports this view?","choices":["Oligodendrocytes come from the notochord","White matter is made by the floor plate","Fewer neurons mean fewer axons, so the loss shows mainly as less myelinated white matter","Neurons themselves make most of the brain's myelin","Myelin forms before neurons are born"],"answer":2,"why":"A neuron deficit means fewer axons for oligodendrocytes to myelinate, so it can appear mainly as reduced white matter (s332-over). The chapter adds that glia outnumber neurons 8 to 1; modern counts put the ratio near 1:1 (see the correction box)."},
  {"id":"c332-ov07","tag":"Neurulation","q":"A neuroscience student studies a growth factor that regulates proliferation, survival, migration and outgrowth of developing neurons and also adult synaptic plasticity for memory. Which factor fits this description in the chapter?","choices":["Brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF)","Retinoic acid","Thalidomide","Folic acid","Valproic acid"],"answer":0,"why":"IGF-I and BDNF regulate many processes from proliferation to the synaptic plasticity of learning and memory (s332-over)."},
  {"id":"c332-ov08","tag":"Neurulation","q":"An embryology student labels a diagram showing cells from the neural fold tips settling between the tube and the overlying skin. Which of these is a product of those cells after ventromedial migration?","choices":["Medulla","Cerebellum","Dorsal root sensory ganglia","Hippocampus","Substantia nigra"],"answer":2,"why":"Neural crest cells migrating ventromedially form dorsal root ganglia, sympathetic chains and enteric ganglia (s332-over, Figure 33-5)."},
  {"id":"c332-rg01","tag":"Regional differentiation","q":"In an experimental embryo, the roof plate is removed early. Researchers then examine the brain. Which finding is most expected?","choices":["Loss of ventral motor neurons","Extra substantia nigra dopamine neurons","Failure of mesoderm formation","Absent notochord","Absent cerebellum and missing midline hippocampal structures"],"answer":4,"why":"Without a roof plate, dorsal structures such as the cerebellum fail to form and midline hippocampal structures are missing (s332-region)."},
  {"id":"c332-rg02","tag":"Regional differentiation","q":"A lab studying Parkinson disease wants to grow substantia nigra dopamine neurons from midbrain precursors. Which embryonic signal, released by the floor plate, should they include?","choices":["Thalidomide","Bone morphogenetic proteins","Retinoic acid","Sonic hedgehog","Folic acid"],"answer":3,"why":"Floor plate Shh, with other factors, induces midbrain precursors to become dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra (s332-region)."},
  {"id":"c332-rg03","tag":"Regional differentiation","q":"A resident reviewing an MRI of a child with a thalamic malformation asks which embryonic division gave rise to the affected structure. What is the answer?","choices":["Telencephalon","Diencephalon","Metencephalon","Mesencephalon","Myelencephalon"],"answer":1,"why":"The diencephalon forms the thalamus and hypothalamus (s332-region)."},
  {"id":"c332-rg04","tag":"Regional differentiation","q":"A 3-year-old has a congenital malformation limited to the pons and cerebellum. A student wants to name the embryonic subdivision these come from. Which is correct?","choices":["Mesencephalon","Prosencephalon","Telencephalon","Diencephalon","Metencephalon"],"answer":4,"why":"The rhombencephalon yields the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) and the myelencephalon (medulla) (s332-region)."},
  {"id":"c332-rg05","tag":"Regional differentiation","q":"A student sees that ventricular zone precursors in two brain domains look identical under the microscope, yet produce different neuron types. What best explains this?","choices":["They are derived from neural crest","They migrate before dividing","They express a checkerboard of molecular genetic determinants","They lack growth factor receptors","They come from different germ layers"],"answer":2,"why":"VZ precursors appear homogeneous but express a checkerboard array of determinants that control which neurons each domain makes (s332-region, Figure 33-6)."},
  {"id":"c332-pz01","tag":"Proliferation and migration","q":"A neuroscience student labels a cortical section. She finds a thin outermost layer formed by early-born cells that came from the earliest preplate. Which cells make up this layer?","choices":["Dentate granule cells","Subplate neurons","Layer 5 pyramidal neurons","Cajal-Retzius cells","Bergmann glia"],"answer":3,"why":"The preplate holds Cajal-Retzius cells, which form the outermost layer 1 (marginal zone), and subplate neurons, which lie under future layer 6 (s332-prolif; Figure 33-7)."},
  {"id":"c332-pz02","tag":"Proliferation and migration","q":"A researcher traces axons from a group of cortical pyramidal neurons and finds they end in the thalamus, brainstem and spinal cord. In which cortical layers do these neurons most likely sit?","choices":["Layer 2","Layer 1","Layer 3","Layers 5 and 6","The marginal zone"],"answer":3,"why":"Deeper layer 5 and 6 neurons project mainly to subcortical targets; layer 3 neurons connect within and between hemispheres (s332-prolif)."},
  {"id":"c332-pz03","tag":"Proliferation and migration","q":"In a tracing study of embryonic rodent forebrain, labeled GABA-secreting cells born in the ventral forebrain are later found in the neocortex. They did not travel along cortical radial glia. Where were these cells most likely born?","choices":["The subgranular zone","The dorsal forebrain ventricular zone","The ganglionic eminences","The external germinal cell layer","The fourth ventricle ventricular zone"],"answer":2,"why":"Most cortical GABA interneurons come from ganglionic eminence precursors and reach the cortex by tangential migration, not along VZ radial glia (s332-prolif; Figure 33-7)."},
  {"id":"c332-pz04","tag":"Proliferation and migration","q":"A psychiatry resident reviews a postmortem study of prefrontal cortex from patients with schizophrenia. The tissue shows fewer interneurons in layer 2 and fewer NOS-expressing neurons. Which other finding does the chapter describe as a possible compensation?","choices":["Loss of Cajal-Retzius cells","Absent radial glia","Excess layer 5 pyramidal neurons","Enlarged subplate zone","Increased GABA-A receptor binding"],"answer":4,"why":"The chapter reports upregulated GABA-A receptor binding in schizophrenia prefrontal cortex, a potential functional compensation (s332-prolif)."},
  {"id":"c332-pz05","tag":"Proliferation and migration","q":"A student compares birth-dating results across brain regions. In one region, first-born neurons lie at the surface and the last-born lie toward the center. Which region fits this pattern?","choices":["Prefrontal cortex layer 2","Neocortical layer 6","Hypothalamus","Neocortex","Cortical plate"],"answer":2,"why":"The hypothalamus, spinal cord and dentate gyrus show outside-to-inside generation, the reverse of the cortex's inside-to-outside pattern (s332-prolif)."},
  {"id":"c332-pz06","tag":"Proliferation and migration","q":"A 9-month-old infant receives a course of steroids. A pediatric neurology fellow worries about a brain cell population still being generated after birth. Which population is she most concerned about?","choices":["Hippocampal CA1 pyramidal cells","Cerebellar Purkinje cells","Cajal-Retzius cells","Deep cerebellar nuclei neurons","Cerebellar granule neurons"],"answer":4,"why":"Cerebellar granule neurogenesis continues postnatally from the EGL, so it is vulnerable to proliferation-inhibiting drugs such as steroids; Purkinje and deep nuclei neurons are born prenatally (s332-prolif)."},
  {"id":"c332-pz07","tag":"Proliferation and migration","q":"A 6-year-old boy has a cerebellar tumor that arises from granule cell precursors that have lost proliferative control. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Huntington disease","Leukodystrophy","Lissencephaly","Multiple sclerosis","Medulloblastoma"],"answer":4,"why":"Loss of proliferative control in the cerebellar granule stem cell population occurs in medulloblastoma, a common childhood brain tumor (s332-prolif; Figure 33-8)."},
  {"id":"c332-pz08","tag":"Proliferation and migration","q":"A researcher studies adult rats after induced seizures and sees more dividing precursors just beneath the dentate gyrus. Which zone is she observing?","choices":["The subgranular zone","The external germinal cell layer","The preplate","The marginal zone","The intermediate zone"],"answer":0,"why":"The SGZ lies just under the dentate gyrus; in rodents its precursors proliferate after ischemia, injury, seizures and growth factors (s332-prolif)."},
  {"id":"c332-cd01","tag":"Cell death","q":"An embryology lecturer describes cell death that separates the digits from the embryonic hand paddle and helps form the optic vesicles. Which type of developmental cell death is this?","choices":["Excitotoxic","Morphogenetic","Necrotic","Phylogenetic","Histogenetic"],"answer":1,"why":"Morphogenetic cell death sculpts the fingers and is needed to form the optic vesicles and the caudal neural tube (s332-death)."},
  {"id":"c332-cd02","tag":"Cell death","q":"A 3-year-old with a history of heavy prenatal alcohol exposure has developmental delay. Her mother asks how alcohol harmed the developing brain. Which mechanism does the chapter link to fetal alcohol syndrome?","choices":["Loss of caspase genes","Failure to inhibit apoptosis","Excess granule cell proliferation","Massive apoptotic cell death","Absent tangential migration"],"answer":3,"why":"The chapter lists fetal alcohol syndrome with hypoxia-ischemia, radiation and neurotoxicants as acquired injuries with massive apoptosis (s332-death)."},
  {"id":"c332-cd03","tag":"Cell death","q":"A medical student reads that in embryonic mice lacking caspase genes, the brain is abnormal. What brain finding should she expect?","choices":["Enlarged, disorganized brain","Normal size with extra synapses","Small, smooth brain","Missing cerebellum only","Normal brain with absent tail"],"answer":0,"why":"Mouse embryos without caspases develop large, disordered brains, with effects that differ clearly by region (s332-death)."},
  {"id":"c332-cd04","tag":"Cell death","q":"A resident explains a classic model in which new neurons survive only if their axons reach the right target and obtain neurotrophins. Which observation shows this model is overly simple?","choices":["Trophic factors have no role in cell survival","Cell death happens only after birth","Neurons never compete for targets","Precursors and immature neurons die before forming synapses","All neurons survive in the cortex"],"answer":3,"why":"Developmental death also strikes precursors and immature neurons before synapses form; trophic interactions do still help control degeneration (s332-death)."},
  {"id":"c332-cd05","tag":"Cell death","q":"A 72-year-old man has progressive memory loss. A student asks which apoptosis disturbance the chapter links to his likely disease group. Which answer fits?","choices":["Excess stimulation of apoptosis in aging","Failure to inhibit apoptosis, as in cancer","Excess cerebellar granule proliferation","Loss of phylogenetic cell death","Absent histogenetic cell death"],"answer":0,"why":"Excess apoptosis occurs in neurodegenerative diseases of aging, such as Alzheimer and Parkinson diseases (s332-death)."},
  {"id":"c332-cd06","tag":"Cell death","q":"A 30-year-old woman has relapsing neurologic symptoms from an autoimmune demyelinating disease. In the chapter's framework, which apoptosis problem is linked to this group of diseases?","choices":["Absent morphogenetic death","Massive apoptosis after hypoxia","Excess apoptosis during development","Excess apoptosis during aging","Failure to inhibit apoptosis"],"answer":4,"why":"Cancers and autoimmune diseases such as multiple sclerosis are linked to apoptosis that is not held in check (s332-death)."},
  {"id":"c332-cd07","tag":"Cell death","q":"A researcher counts neurons in one developing population and finds that most of them are lost before maturity. Which type of developmental cell death can eliminate 20 to 80 percent of the cells in certain neuron groups?","choices":["Traumatic","Histogenetic","Autoimmune","Phylogenetic","Morphogenetic"],"answer":1,"why":"Histogenetic cell death removes selected cells as brain regions develop and can remove 20 to 80 percent of a given neuron group (s332-death)."},
  {"id":"c332-np01","tag":"Patterning principles","q":"A graduate student cultures neural precursors with a growth factor, but cells from one region show no response, while others respond strongly. Staining shows the nonresponding cells lack the factor's receptor. Which concept best explains the difference?","choices":["Experience-dependent cortical specialization","Programmed cell death of precursors","Default neural differentiation","Tangential migration of interneurons","Differences in competence set by patterning genes"],"answer":4,"why":"Competence, the ability to respond, depends on cognate receptor expression, which patterning genes determine (s332-pattern)."},
  {"id":"c332-np02","tag":"Patterning principles","q":"A neuroscience fellow studies mutant mice in which thalamic axons never innervate the cortex. She finds that regional cortical patterning genes are expressed normally. Which idea do her results best support?","choices":["Regional identity resides in a VZ protomap that thalamic input modulates","Cortex begins as a uniform sheet","Areas are specified only after layers form","Thalamic input is required to create cortical areas","Cortical genes depend on visual experience"],"answer":0,"why":"Gbx2 and Mash1 mutants show unaltered cortical patterning genes despite disrupted thalamocortical innervation, supporting an intrinsic protomap modulated by thalamic afferents (s332-pattern)."},
  {"id":"c332-np03","tag":"Patterning principles","q":"A 32-year-old resident reads that a single transcription factor knockout produces defects in neurons, glia and cell migration. A co-resident is surprised that one gene affects so many processes. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["Transcription factors act only after birth","The knockout must also delete neighboring genes","Each factor binds only one promoter","Transcription factors lack DNA-binding domains","The factor acts in multimeric complexes whose effects depend on the cellular environment"],"answer":4,"why":"Factors form multimeric complexes; combinatorial promoter regulation gives diverse outcomes when one gene is altered (s332-pattern)."},
  {"id":"c332-np04","tag":"Patterning principles","q":"A researcher finds that two neighboring forebrain regions each express a factor that suppresses the other's factor. When one factor is deleted, the other's domain expands. Which principle does this illustrate?","choices":["Genetic fate mapping","Default neural fate","Gain of function","Cross-regulation establishing tissue boundaries","Competence"],"answer":3,"why":"Cross-regulatory repression between factors establishes tissue boundaries, such as basal ganglia versus cortex (s332-pattern)."},
  {"id":"c332-np05","tag":"Patterning principles","q":"A lab permanently marks all cells that once expressed Wnt3a and follows them into adulthood. Labeled cells are found across the diencephalon, midbrain, brainstem and rostral spinal cord. Most labeled structures belong to which network?","choices":["The motor system","The limbic system","The auditory system","The olfactory system","The visual system"],"answer":2,"why":"Genetic fate mapping of Wnt3a-expressing cells showed that most of the structures they contributed to form the auditory system (s332-pattern)."},
  {"id":"c332-np06","tag":"Patterning principles","q":"A student overexpresses a patterning gene in a forebrain region where it is normally absent and records the resulting defects. Which experimental approach is this?","choices":["Loss of function","Surgical rewiring","Promoter polymorphism analysis","Ectopic gain of function","Genetic fate mapping"],"answer":3,"why":"Over- or ectopic expression is gain of function; together with loss-of-function deletions it was used to define patterning hierarchies (s332-pattern)."},
  {"id":"c332-sc01","tag":"Spinal cord patterning","q":"In an embryology experiment, a researcher dissociates early ectoderm so that cells no longer receive BMP signaling from neighbors. Most cells become neural. Which principle explains this?","choices":["Noggin promotes epidermis","BMPs are required for neural induction","Shh converts ectoderm into neurons","Neural fate is the default when BMP signaling is absent","Retinoic acid induces neural fate dorsally"],"answer":3,"why":"BMPs maintain epidermis and block neural fate; neural differentiation is the default unless inhibited (s332-cord)."},
  {"id":"c332-sc02","tag":"Spinal cord patterning","q":"A medical student studies the chick embryo and grafts tissue from the front (rostral) tip of the primitive streak, which secretes noggin, follistatin and chordin. What is this tissue?","choices":["Anterior neural ridge","Hensen's node","Cortical hem","Roof plate","Floor plate"],"answer":1,"why":"Hensen's node, the counterpart of the Spemann organizer, sits at the front tip of the primitive streak and secretes BMP inhibitors (s332-cord)."},
  {"id":"c332-sc03","tag":"Spinal cord patterning","q":"A developmental biologist removes the notochord and floor plate from an early spinal cord explant. Which cell types are most likely to fail to form?","choices":["Sensory relay interneurons","Neural crest cells","Motoneurons and ventral interneurons","Dorsal root sensory neurons","Roof plate cells"],"answer":2,"why":"Shh from the notochord and floor plate drives ventral motoneuron and interneuron fates (s332-cord)."},
  {"id":"c332-sc04","tag":"Spinal cord patterning","q":"In a mouse study, ventral spinal progenitors exposed to high Shh show loss of a set of transcription factors normally found more dorsally. Which gene is most likely among those repressed?","choices":["Nkx2.2","Isl1","Hnf3β","Nkx6.1","Irx3"],"answer":4,"why":"High Shh induces Hnf3β, Nkx6.1 and Nkx2.2 but shuts off Pax6, Pax7, Irx3 and Dbx1/2, genes of dorsal domains; Isl1 is induced in motoneurons (s332-cord)."},
  {"id":"c332-sc05","tag":"Spinal cord patterning","q":"A researcher blocks Shh signaling after motoneurons have formed. Ventral interneurons still differentiate and express En1 or Lim1/2. What does this best show?","choices":["En1 is induced by BMPs","Interneurons derive from neural crest","Ventral interneuron differentiation at this stage is independent of Shh","Motoneurons require En1","Shh represses En1"],"answer":2,"why":"Once motoneurons form, ventral interneurons turn on En1 or Lim1/2 without Shh signaling (s332-cord)."},
  {"id":"c332-sc06","tag":"Spinal cord patterning","q":"A student studying hindbrain segments finds that blocking an anterior signal that acts upstream of hox genes disrupts the segmental pattern. Which signal was most likely blocked?","choices":["BMP","Retinoic acid","Shh","Noggin","TGF-α"],"answer":1,"why":"Retinoic acid works anteriorly and sits upstream of hox genes; FGFs act posteriorly in rostrocaudal patterning (s332-cord)."},
  {"id":"c332-cx01","tag":"Forebrain patterning","q":"A ferret pup has its lateral geniculate nucleus removed shortly after birth. As an adult, it responds to visual stimuli, and recordings show visual responses in auditory cortex. What does this best illustrate?","choices":["The developing cortex is flexible in mediating modality-specific functions","Visual cortex regenerates after injury","Cortical areas are fixed by the protomap and cannot change","Auditory cortex is derived from the hippocampus","Thalamic axons never reach the cortex"],"answer":0,"why":"In the ferret, visual signaling transferred to the auditory cortex, which successfully mediated vision, showing unexpected cortical flexibility (s332-cortex)."},
  {"id":"c332-cx02","tag":"Forebrain patterning","q":"At journal club, one resident argues that cortex starts with no functional areas until thalamic axons arrive; another argues intrinsic molecular labels decide everything, including thalamic targeting. Based on the experiments in the chapter, which conclusion is best?","choices":["Both models were abandoned for the prosomere model","The protomap model is correct","Thalamic axons arrive before the cortex forms","Neither model is entirely correct","The protocortex model is correct"],"answer":3,"why":"Early molecular regionalization exists, but initial thalamic targeting is independent of it; later local cues refine connections, so neither model is entirely correct (s332-cortex)."},
  {"id":"c332-cx03","tag":"Forebrain patterning","q":"A mouse knockout shows an absent hippocampus while the adjacent cerebral cortex looks largely normal. The deleted gene is normally expressed in the cortical hem and encodes a soluble growth factor. Which gene was most likely deleted?","choices":["Wnt3a","Nkx2.1","Emx2","Pax6","Gsh2"],"answer":0,"why":"Mice lacking Wnt3a, expressed in the cortical hem, have a missing or reduced hippocampus with cortex mainly preserved (s332-cortex)."},
  {"id":"c332-cx04","tag":"Forebrain patterning","q":"A researcher deletes a cortical hem gene in mice. The hem and choroid plexus are both missing, hem cells seem to overproliferate, and the hippocampal primordium is present but disorganized. Which gene was deleted?","choices":["Wnt3a","Lhx5","Nkx2.1","Lef1","NeuroD"],"answer":1,"why":"Lhx5 deletion removes the hem and neighboring choroid plexus, with possible excess hem proliferation and disorganized hippocampal primordia (s332-cortex)."},
  {"id":"c332-cx05","tag":"Forebrain patterning","q":"Mice with a mutation in a bHLH transcription factor show defective differentiation of one hippocampal subregion. Which subregion is most likely affected?","choices":["Dentate gyrus","Fornix","Entorhinal cortex","Subiculum","CA1"],"answer":0,"why":"NeuroD and Mash1 (bHLH) mutants show faulty dentate gyrus differentiation (s332-cortex)."},
  {"id":"c332-cx06","tag":"Forebrain patterning","q":"A postdoctoral fellow deletes a gene expressed mainly in the medial ganglionic eminence. LGE markers spread ventrally and neocortical and striatal GABA interneurons fall by half. Which gene was deleted?","choices":["Gsh2","Pax6","Emx2","Nkx2.1","Dlx2"],"answer":3,"why":"Nkx2.1 deletion lets LGE factor expression spread into MGE territory and neocortical and striatal GABA interneurons fall by 50 percent (s332-cortex)."},
  {"id":"c332-cx07","tag":"Forebrain patterning","q":"A student traces labeled cells from the ventral forebrain ganglionic eminences into the developing neocortex. Which adult cell type do these migrating cells mainly become?","choices":["Radial glia","GABA interneurons","Pyramidal projection neurons","Choroid plexus cells","Thalamic relay neurons"],"answer":1,"why":"The basal ganglia anlage appears to give rise to virtually all adult GABA interneurons, which reach the neocortex by tangential migration (s332-cortex)."},
  {"id":"c332-cx08","tag":"Forebrain patterning","q":"In a mouse mutant, dorsal cortical markers spread ventrally and olfactory bulb interneurons are reduced. The deleted factor is normally expressed in the lateral ganglionic eminence. Which factor is it?","choices":["Pax6","Nkx2.1","Lhx2","Gsh2","Hnf3β"],"answer":3,"why":"Gsh2 is the main LGE factor; its deletion leads to ventral expansion of dorsal cortical markers and fewer olfactory interneurons (s332-cortex)."},
  {"id":"c332-ns01","tag":"Specification and miRNAs","q":"A graduate student deletes Math1 in mice and finds too few granule neurons in the dentate gyrus. She wonders which other factor, if deleted, would give a similar deficit. Which factor is it most likely to be?","choices":["NeuroD","Otx1","Id","Olig1/2","Reelin"],"answer":0,"why":"Hippocampal granule neuron fate depends on both NeuroD and Math1; deleting either one leaves deficient cell numbers (Neuronal specification)."},
  {"id":"c332-ns02","tag":"Specification and miRNAs","q":"A neurology resident reviews a study showing that one miRNA, normally present in midbrain dopamine neurons, is reduced in midbrain samples from patients with a movement disorder. Which miRNA and disease fit this finding?","choices":["miR-132 in schizophrenia","miR-9 in Alzheimer disease","miR-219 in a tauopathy","miR-124a in brain cancer","miR-133b in Parkinson disease"],"answer":4,"why":"The chapter reports miR-133b is found in midbrain dopaminergic neurons and is lacking in Parkinson disease midbrain tissue (miRNA section)."},
  {"id":"c332-ns03","tag":"Specification and miRNAs","q":"A medical student studying Figure 33-11 asks which step occurs inside the nucleus before the miRNA precursor is exported. Which step is it?","choices":["Drosha cleavage of the primary miRNA hairpin","Loading of the guide strand into RISC","Dicer cleavage of the stem loop","Argonaute degradation of the mRNA duplex","Pairing of miRNA with target mRNA"],"answer":0,"why":"Drosha, with Pasha in the Microprocessor, cuts the primary miRNA in the nucleus; the pre-miRNA then leaves through nuclear pores, and the other steps happen in the cytoplasm (Figure 33-11)."},
  {"id":"c332-ns04","tag":"Specification and miRNAs","q":"A researcher finds that a bHLH factor drives oligodendrocyte development in one brain region but motor neuron differentiation elsewhere. She asks what best explains these different outcomes. What is the best explanation?","choices":["The factor is a noncoding RNA","The factor is translated only in glia","Cell fate depends on the combination of factors each cell expresses","Different regions secrete different amounts of reelin","Motor neurons arise from the rostral migratory stream"],"answer":2,"why":"Olig1/2 shows that the variety of factors a cell expresses produces combinatorial effects and diverse fates (Neuronal specification)."},
  {"id":"c332-ns05","tag":"Specification and miRNAs","q":"A geriatric psychiatry fellow reads that a group of miRNAs abundant in fetal hippocampus is differentially regulated with aging. In which condition does the chapter report these miRNAs are altered in the hippocampus?","choices":["Alzheimer disease","Williams syndrome","Miller-Dieker syndrome","Medulloblastoma","Autism spectrum disorder"],"answer":0,"why":"the set miR-9, miR-124a and miR-125b, plus miR-132, miR-128 and miR-219, shows changes in the Alzheimer disease hippocampus (miRNA section)."},
  {"id":"c332-ns06","tag":"Specification and miRNAs","q":"A lab wants to lower the level of one specific protein in cultured neurons to study its role in development, using a tool first discovered in plants. Which tool fits best?","choices":["Short-interfering RNA (siRNA)","Netrin","Reelin antibody","Basic FGF","Messenger RNA overexpression"],"answer":0,"why":"siRNAs, found in plants, are now used in research to downregulate specific gene products and study neurodevelopmental pathways (miRNA section)."},
  {"id":"c332-ef01","tag":"Extracellular factors","q":"A researcher injects a factor into the cerebral ventricles of embryonic rats. The adults have a larger cortex with many more glutamatergic neurons, while GABA neuron numbers are unchanged. Which factor was most likely injected?","choices":["bFGF","GABA","PACAP","TGF-beta","TNF-alpha"],"answer":0,"why":"Embryonic ICV bFGF produced 87 percent more neurons, all glutamatergic, with GABA neurons unchanged; the other choices inhibit proliferation (Extracellular factors, Table 33-1)."},
  {"id":"c332-ef02","tag":"Extracellular factors","q":"In an embryonic rat experiment, a peptide injection lowers cortical precursor proliferation by 26 percent, and fewer labeled layer 5/6 neurons are found 5 days later. Which peptide was used?","choices":["Shh","EGF","PACAP","bFGF","IGF-I"],"answer":2,"why":"PACAP is antimitogenic in the cortical VZ; embryonic PACAP injection cut proliferation by 26 percent (Extracellular factors)."},
  {"id":"c332-ef03","tag":"Extracellular factors","q":"A neonatologist asks about a study in which one injection of a growth factor under the skin of newborn rats raised dentate gyrus proliferation twofold within 8 hours. Which mechanism of action does this finding support?","choices":["Autocrine","Paracrine","Endocrine","Synaptic","Intracrine"],"answer":2,"why":"A peripheral bFGF injection reached the CSF and stimulated proliferation, consistent with an endocrine mechanism (signals from the general circulation)."},
  {"id":"c332-ef04","tag":"Extracellular factors","q":"A premature infant receives perinatal medication to promote lung maturation. A resident asks what is known about how this lipid-soluble drug class affects neurogenesis. What does the chapter report?","choices":["It cannot cross the BBB","It crosses the BBB and inhibits neurogenesis; its impact on human brain formation is unstudied","It has been proven safe for human brain formation","It acts only on glial survival","It stimulates neurogenesis only in newborns"],"answer":1,"why":"Steroids cross the BBB due to lipid solubility and inhibit neurogenesis across the age spectrum, but no one has yet studied how they affect the forming human brain (Clinical implications)."},
  {"id":"c332-ef05","tag":"Extracellular factors","q":"A 4-year-old has a cerebellar tumor. His parents, who read about rat granule cell studies, ask which developmental signals are involved. Disruption of which pair of signals does the chapter link to human medulloblastoma?","choices":["Reelin and Dab1","Netrin and Slit","Shh and PACAP","EGF and LIF","BDNF and NT3"],"answer":2,"why":"Shh and PACAP regulate cerebellar granule neurogenesis, and their disruption contributes to human medulloblastoma (Extracellular factors)."},
  {"id":"c332-ef06","tag":"Extracellular factors","q":"A 29-year-old pregnant woman has a serious infection in the second trimester. She asks whether this could affect her baby's later mental health. Which psychiatric disorder does the chapter name as having maternal infection as a known risk factor?","choices":["Williams syndrome","Parkinson disease","Miller-Dieker syndrome","Medulloblastoma","Schizophrenia"],"answer":4,"why":"Infection in the mother while pregnant is an established schizophrenia risk factor; placental cytokines affect fetal brain development in animal models (Clinical implications)."},
  {"id":"c332-ef07","tag":"Extracellular factors","q":"In a rat experiment, bFGF is injected at a late developmental age when gliogenesis predominates. What effect is expected?","choices":["Loss of Purkinje cells","A selective change in glial numbers","Inverted cortical lamination","No effect at any later age","An increase in GABA interneurons only"],"answer":1,"why":"Mitogen effects depend on the stage of regional development; late bFGF selectively affected glial numbers (Extracellular factors)."},
  {"id":"c332-ef08","tag":"Extracellular factors","q":"A student reviewing Table 33-1 asks which extracellular factor increases survival of immature neurons but decreases survival of mature neurons. Which factor is it?","choices":["Glutamate","BDNF","bFGF","IGF-1","TNF-alpha"],"answer":0,"why":"Table 33-1 lists glutamate as raising survival of immature neurons and lowering survival of mature neurons."},
  {"id":"c332-ef09","tag":"Extracellular factors","q":"Mouse embryonic stem cells are cultured under conditions that block their own Shh signaling. They produce neurons with the features of cortical pyramidal cells that, when grafted, project to visual, limbic and subcortical targets. What does this suggest?","choices":["Forebrain specification may be an intrinsic program that emerges without extrinsic signals","Cortical identity depends on reelin alone","Grafted cells cannot form projections","Shh is required to make cortical neurons","Pyramidal neurons come only from the rhombic lip"],"answer":0,"why":"The chapter reports this study suggests embryonic forebrain specification may be an ancestral intrinsic program arising without extrinsic signals (Extracellular factors)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg01","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"A 17-year-old has focal seizures. MRI shows a small cluster of gray matter in the wrong location deep to the cortex. What developmental process most likely failed?","choices":["Axonal fasciculation","Synaptic pruning","Neural tube closure","Neuronal migration, producing a heterotopia","Myelination"],"answer":3,"why":"Incomplete migration of restricted neuron groups (heterotopias) frequently underlies focal seizure disorders (Cell migration)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg02","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"An infant has a smooth brain surface lacking gyri and a deletion at chromosome 17q13.3. Which gene, a human homolog of the fungal gene NudF, is most likely involved?","choices":["DCX","Filamin 1","LIS-1","c-Met","RELN"],"answer":2,"why":"Miller-Dieker syndrome is lissencephaly with an abnormal 17q13.3; the gene is LIS-1 (PAFAH1B1), homolog of NudF (Cell migration)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg03","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"A 21-year-old woman has epilepsy and intellectual impairment. MRI shows a band of neurons in the white matter beneath the cortex, appearing as a double cortex. Her brother had lissencephaly. Which gene is most likely mutated?","choices":["Filamin 1","Neuregulin","LIS-1","DCX","RELN"],"answer":3,"why":"DCX mutations cause X-linked lissencephaly in males and subcortical heterotopic bands (double cortex) in females (Cell migration)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg04","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"A child with an autosomal recessive brain malformation has a markedly thick cortex with pachygyria, abnormal hippocampi and severe cerebellar hypoplasia lacking folia. Which gene is most likely involved?","choices":["Filamin 1","LIS-1","LIMK","DCX","RELN"],"answer":4,"why":"Human RELN mutations are tied to an autosomal recessive lissencephaly with cerebellar hypoplasia with these features (Cell migration)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg05","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"A 30-year-old woman has seizures, and MRI shows nodules of gray matter lining the lateral ventricles. Mutation of which gene, coding an actin-associated protein, is most likely?","choices":["DCX","Astrotactin","Filamin 1","RELN","LIS-1"],"answer":2,"why":"Filamin 1 mutations cause periventricular nodular heterotopias in humans (Cell migration)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg06","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"A newborn with congenital muscular dystrophy has severe eye abnormalities and a brain surface that is partly smooth and partly pebbled with poor lamination. What type of brain malformation is this?","choices":["Medulloblastoma","Periventricular nodular heterotopia","Classical type I lissencephaly","Double cortex","Cobblestone type II lissencephaly"],"answer":4,"why":"Defects in mannose-transferring enzymes in some congenital muscular dystrophies produce type II cobblestone lissencephaly (Cell migration)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg07","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"A researcher studies a mouse mutant whose cortical neurons are layered outside-to-inside, the reverse of normal. Which protein, made by Cajal-Retzius cells, is most likely missing?","choices":["Netrin","Filamin 1","Doublecortin","Reelin","Astrotactin"],"answer":3,"why":"The reeler mouse has inverted cortical lamination; reelin is a secreted glycoprotein made by Cajal-Retzius cells (Cell migration)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg08","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"A child with Williams syndrome has a characteristic language profile. A geneticist explains that one implicated gene codes for an actin-modulating kinase used in growth cone movement. Which kinase is it?","choices":["CDK5","ErbB2","c-Met","LIMK","Eph A3"],"answer":3,"why":"LIMK kinase, involved in rapid growth cone movements, is responsible for the language phenotype of Williams syndrome (Differentiation and outgrowth)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg09","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"In an experiment, retinal ganglion cell axons arising in the posterior retina carry high Eph A3 levels and grow toward the optic tectum. Where will they preferentially terminate?","choices":["Posterior tectum, where ephrin is high","Evenly across the tectum","The floor plate","Anterior tectum, where ephrin is low","The anterior commissure"],"answer":3,"why":"The tectum has ephrin A2/A5 highest posteriorly; high-Eph axons avoid high ephrin, which triggers growth cone retraction, so they innervate the anterior tectum (Differentiation and outgrowth)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg10","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"A student asks how spinothalamic sensory interneuron axons are drawn toward the anterior commissure. Which secreted floor plate molecule acts mainly as a chemoattractant here?","choices":["Slit","Hepatocyte growth factor","Ephrin A5","Semaphorin","Netrin"],"answer":4,"why":"Netrins are primarily chemoattractants secreted by the floor plate; Slit, ephrins, semaphorins and HGF act as repellents in the chapter (Differentiation and outgrowth)."},
  {"id":"c332-mg11","tag":"Migration and outgrowth","q":"A child psychiatry fellow reads that variation in a receptor gene for hepatocyte growth factor has been associated with autism spectrum disorder. Which developmental process is this receptor thought to affect?","choices":["Myelination by oligodendrocytes","Proliferation in the rostral migratory stream","Tangential migration of GABA interneurons into the cortex","Neural tube closure","Radial migration of Purkinje cells"],"answer":2,"why":"HGF and its c-Met receptor push GABA interneurons away from the basal forebrain and into the cortex; c-Met is associated with ASDs (Cell migration)."},
  {"id":"c332-nb01","tag":"Neurodevelopmental basis","q":"A neuropathology fellow examines cortex from a 45-year-old man. The layering is normal, but neurons are smaller than usual and look immature. There is no gliosis and no white blood cell infiltrate. How would a neuropathologist classically label this picture?","choices":["Acute ischemic injury","Reactive gliosis","Arrest of development","Neuronal heterotopia","Neurodegeneration"],"answer":2,"why":"Normal cytoarchitecture with small, immature-looking neurons and no inflammation is termed an arrest of development; adding gliosis and white cell infiltrate would make it neurodegeneration (s332-basis)."},
  {"id":"c332-nb02","tag":"Neurodevelopmental basis","q":"A 70-year-old woman with Parkinson disease dies, and her autopsy shows small neurons with gliosis and a white blood cell infiltrate. A resident asks which glial cell type the chapter links with many neurodegenerative processes such as this one. Which is it?","choices":["Radial glia","Schwann cells","Ependymal cells","Microglia","Oligodendrocytes"],"answer":3,"why":"The chapter links microglia with many degenerative processes, Alzheimer and Parkinson diseases among them (s332-basis)."},
  {"id":"c332-nb03","tag":"Neurodevelopmental basis","q":"A research group blocks BDNF signaling in the brains of adult rats. Months later, cortical neurons and their dendrites have shrunk. Which additional finding best matches what the chapter reports for this model?","choices":["Increased cortical neuron number","Marked astrocyte proliferation","Widespread neuronal heterotopias","No glial cell proliferation","Dense white blood cell infiltrate"],"answer":3,"why":"In animal models, interrupting adult BDNF signaling causes neuron and dendrite atrophy without glial cell proliferation, showing that adult shrinkage can occur without inflammation (s332-basis)."},
  {"id":"c332-nb04","tag":"Neurodevelopmental basis","q":"A postmortem study of adults with autism finds small cortical neurons with no gliosis. A journal club presenter concludes the disorder must be purely developmental. What is the best response based on the chapter?","choices":["The conclusion is correct because gliosis is always absent in developmental disorders","The absence of gliosis proves the cause is an adult injury","Adult neurons can shrink without inflammation, so the finding does not prove a developmental origin","Small neurons always indicate active neurodegeneration","Neuron size cannot be measured postmortem"],"answer":2,"why":"Because adult neuronal shrinkage can occur without inflammation (for example after adult BDNF loss in animals), small neurons without gliosis do not mean a condition is only or primarily developmental (s332-basis)."},
  {"id":"c332-nb05","tag":"Neurodevelopmental basis","q":"A 60-year-old patient's oncologist and psychiatrist discuss which developmental process, still active in adults, protects against tumors as cells accumulate mutations over life. Which process does the chapter credit with this role?","choices":["Myelination","Synaptogenesis","Gliogenesis","Axonal retraction","Programmed cell death"],"answer":4,"why":"The chapter calls programmed cell death key to stopping tumor formation as mutations build up over a lifetime (s332-basis)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz01","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"Researchers infect pregnant mice with human influenza, causing pneumonia. The offspring show altered brain development, yet no virus is found in the fetus or placenta. What does the chapter propose as the likely mediator?","choices":["Maternal inflammatory cytokine response","Paternal genetic transmission","Direct viral infection of fetal neurons","Maternal antiviral drug exposure","Fetal hypoxia from placental infarction"],"answer":0,"why":"In this animal model, the mother's inflammatory cytokine response may directly affect offspring brain development, with no evidence of virus in fetus or placenta (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz02","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"A 24-year-old man with schizophrenia has serial MRIs over 6 years showing increasing ventricular size and cortical gray matter loss. Pathology in similar cases shows no inflammatory cells. How should these progressive changes best be interpreted according to the chapter?","choices":["They rule out any developmental contribution","They prove schizophrenia is a classic neurodegenerative disease","They are imaging artifacts with no meaning","They indicate a superimposed viral encephalitis","They should prompt reconsideration of active degeneration, possibly from the illness or from what follows it, for example stress or drug treatment"],"answer":4,"why":"The chapter says ongoing progressive changes should lead to reconsidering active degeneration, whether from the illness or what follows it, though classic inflammatory signs are absent (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz03","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"The healthy 22-year-old sister of a woman with schizophrenia has a research MRI. Her hippocampal volume is smaller than that of normal controls and close to her sister's. What does this finding most likely represent?","choices":["Prodromal schizophrenia that will certainly progress","A pathognomonic sign of schizophrenia","A normal variant unrelated to schizophrenia risk","A marker of genetic susceptibility","Evidence of hippocampal neurodegeneration"],"answer":3,"why":"Healthy genetically at-risk individuals have hippocampal volumes closer to affected probands; the reduction is not pathognomonic and may be a biologic marker of genetic susceptibility (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz04","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"Parents of a 17-year-old boy recently diagnosed with schizophrenia ask about his identical twin brother. Approximately what proportion of monozygotic twin pairs are both affected, according to the chapter?","choices":["70 to 80 percent","5 to 10 percent","15 to 20 percent","Nearly 100 percent","40 to 50 percent"],"answer":4,"why":"In only 40 to 50 percent of identical twin pairs are both twins ill, showing genes alone do not ensure disease and the embryonic environment contributes (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz05","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"A postmortem study reports normal neuron density in the hippocampus of patients with schizophrenia, and the authors claim no neurons are lost. They did not measure the total volume of the region. What is the main limitation of their conclusion?","choices":["Hippocampal neurons cannot be counted after death","Density should be measured only in white matter","Without total regional volume, density cannot reveal population size","Schizophrenia affects only glial cells","Density studies always overestimate cell number"],"answer":2,"why":"Most studies assess only regional density; without a region's total volume, density alone cannot reveal population size (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz06","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"A researcher stains cortex from patients with schizophrenia for interneuron markers. Which pattern does the chapter report?","choices":["Pyramidal cells reduced, all interneurons normal","Parvalbumin cells reduced, calretinin cells normal","Both subtypes absent","Both subtypes increased","Calretinin cells reduced, parvalbumin cells normal"],"answer":1,"why":"Interneurons carrying parvalbumin are decreased while those with calretinin are spared, pointing to loss of one subtype (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz07","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"A 50-year-old man with long-standing bipolar disorder with psychotic symptoms dies, and his brain is studied. Which protein, first expressed by Cajal-Retzius cells in the embryo and later by adult GABA neurons, would the chapter predict to be reduced by 30 to 50 percent?","choices":["Reelin","Synaptophysin","Parvalbumin","GAD67","Calretinin"],"answer":0,"why":"Reelin, first made by Cajal-Retzius cells and later by adult GABA neurons, falls by 30 to 50 percent in psychotic bipolar disorder and in schizophrenia (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz08","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"A neuropathologist examining the brain of a 38-year-old woman with schizophrenia finds clusters of neurons in entorhinal cortex lamina II and scattered neurons in the white matter under the prefrontal cortex. Which developmental process do these findings most directly implicate?","choices":["Neuronal migration","Adult neurogenesis","Neural tube closure","Myelin breakdown","Microglial activation"],"answer":0,"why":"Misplaced or clustered neurons in entorhinal lamina II and subcortical white matter reflect altered developmental migration, survival and connectivity; the chapter calls this the most compelling developmental evidence (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz09","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"A graduate student studies hippocampal pyramidal neurons in schizophrenia. Compared with controls, which set of findings does the chapter describe?","choices":["Smaller neurons with fewer dendritic branches and spines and reduced neuropil","Doubled neuron number with gliosis","Normal neurons with increased MAP2","Absent pyramidal layer","Larger cell bodies with more spines"],"answer":0,"why":"Pyramidal neurons appear smaller with fewer dendritic arborizations and spines and reduced neuropil, with lower MAP2, spinophilin, synaptophysin and SNAP25 (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz10","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"A lab studies a schizophrenia candidate gene whose protein regulates migration and neurite outgrowth in development, modulates synaptic plasticity in adults, and forms a complex with Lis1 and NudEL downstream of reelin. Which gene is this?","choices":["Gsh2","Lef1","DISC1","DLX1","Nkx2.1"],"answer":2,"why":"DISC1 protein regulates migration, neurite outgrowth and maturation in development, modulates cytoskeleton, transmission and plasticity in adults, and complexes with Lis1 and NudEL downstream of reelin (s332-scz). As a schizophrenia risk gene it was not confirmed by later genome-wide studies (see the update box)."},
  {"id":"c332-sz11","tag":"Schizophrenia","q":"A study finds fewer GABA interneurons in hippocampus and cortex in schizophrenia. A resident asks from which embryonic precursor region these cortical and hippocampal interneurons may mainly derive. What is the best answer?","choices":["Medial ganglionic eminence under Nkx2.1","Lateral ganglionic eminence under Gsh2","Neural crest","Rhombic lip under Math1","Cortical ventricular zone under Lhx"],"answer":0,"why":"Interneurons of the cortex and hippocampus may come mainly from the MGE under Nkx2.1, while olfactory and SVZ neurons arise from LGE precursors expressing Gsh2 (s332-scz)."},
  {"id":"c332-as01","tag":"Autism spectrum","q":"A 2-year-old girl with suspected ASD has a head circumference that was average at birth but has climbed steeply since about 11 months. Her father asks a resident which pattern this matches. Which statement best fits the chapter?","choices":["Brain size is enlarged at birth and normalizes by age 1","Brain growth is normal until age 4 and then accelerates","Brain size is small at birth and catches up by age 2","Brain growth slows after age 1 and stalls by age 4","Brain size is typical at birth and then grows faster than usual through ages 2 to 4"],"answer":4,"why":"The chapter describes average size at birth with accelerated growth from late in year 1 through ages 2 to 4 (s332-asd). Later studies found this overgrowth subtle and limited to a subgroup, so head size is not a reliable marker of autism (see the update box)."},
  {"id":"c332-as02","tag":"Autism spectrum","q":"A neuropathology fellow reviews 29 brains from people with ASD. Most show fewer Purkinje neurons with no gliosis, no empty baskets and no retrograde loss of inferior olive neurons. What do these negative findings most suggest?","choices":["Hippocampal atrophy spreading to the cerebellum","An acquired postnatal injury","A chronic inflammatory process","Seizure-related neuronal loss","A prenatal origin of the Purkinje deficit"],"answer":4,"why":"Absence of markers of acquired postnatal lesions points to a prenatal origin (s332-asd)."},
  {"id":"c332-as03","tag":"Autism spectrum","q":"A 34-year-old man was exposed to thalidomide in utero and has autism. A student asks during which gestational window thalidomide exposure was linked to autism. Which answer is correct?","choices":["Days 40 to 44","Days 20 to 24","Weeks 28 to 30","Weeks 12 to 14","Days 5 to 9"],"answer":1,"why":"Among 15 children exposed on days 20 to 24, when brainstem cranial and Purkinje neurogenesis occurs, four had autism (s332-asd)."},
  {"id":"c332-as04","tag":"Autism spectrum","q":"A researcher studies a gene in children with ASD whose product is the hepatocyte growth factor receptor. Which developmental process is it reported to affect?","choices":["Radial migration of cortical pyramidal neurons","Purkinje cell proliferation in the cerebellum","Myelination of cortical white matter","Neural tube closure","Tangential movement of GABA interneurons out of the ganglionic eminences"],"answer":4,"why":"cMET acts on how GABA interneurons travel tangentially out of the ganglionic eminences of the ventral forebrain, possibly unbalancing excitation and inhibition (s332-asd)."},
  {"id":"c332-as05","tag":"Autism spectrum","q":"A genetics resident explains an ASD-associated polymorphism in a cerebellar patterning gene that, in animal models, causes Purkinje and granule neuron deficits when dysregulated. Which gene is it?","choices":["ENGRAILED-2","cMET","mTOR","Neuroligin","Wnt3a"],"answer":0,"why":"ENGRAILED-2 is the cerebellar patterning gene that controls proliferation and differentiation (s332-asd)."},
  {"id":"c332-as06","tag":"Autism spectrum","q":"A 6-year-old boy with autism has a single-gene syndrome. His neurologist notes that several monogenic causes of autism may share one mechanism. Which mechanism does the chapter propose?","choices":["Failure of neural tube closure","Excess myelination of the corpus callosum","Loss of hippocampal neurons","Overactive HPA axis signaling","Synaptic protein synthesis regulated through PI3K/Akt and mTOR"],"answer":4,"why":"A possible link among monogenic causes is synaptic protein synthesis, mainly via PI3K/Akt and the mTOR complex (s332-asd)."},
  {"id":"c332-as07","tag":"Autism spectrum","q":"A 9-year-old girl with ASD performs normally on evoked cortical potential and oculomotor testing but struggles with complex reasoning. A researcher argues this supports a brain-wide model. Which model is it?","choices":["A purely hippocampal disorder","A lesion confined to primary auditory cortex","A peripheral sensory disorder","A defect limited to the social network","A disorder of synaptic organization present throughout the brain"],"answer":4,"why":"Intact primary sensory perception with disturbed higher processing suggests a synaptic organization disorder spread throughout the brain (s332-asd)."},
  {"id":"c332-as08","tag":"Autism spectrum","q":"A research team reviews brain tissue from children with ASD. They see disordered cortical layers, misoriented pyramidal cells and ectopic neurons in white matter. Which developmental processes do these changes most directly implicate?","choices":["Neural tube closure and segmentation","Cortical neurogenesis and migration","Adult neurogenesis and gliogenesis","Myelination and synaptic pruning","Blood-brain barrier formation"],"answer":1,"why":"These cortical changes point to faulty production and migration of cortical neurons (s332-asd)."},
  {"id":"c332-as09","tag":"Autism spectrum","q":"A student asks why neuropathologic findings in ASD vary so much between brains and regions. Which explanation does the chapter offer?","choices":["Most findings are artifacts of tissue processing","Insults occur at different times and hit each region by its own developmental schedule","All changes arise from a single postnatal infection","Only the hippocampus is truly affected","The differences reflect medication effects"],"answer":1,"why":"The data support developmental abnormalities at different times, each region affected on its own timetable of neurogenesis and differentiation (s332-asd)."},
  {"id":"c332-an01","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A neuroscience student injects adult mice with BrdU and finds labeled neuroblasts moving in chains toward the front of the brain. Where will these cells most likely end up?","choices":["Motor cortex, as pyramidal neurons","Olfactory bulb, as GABA interneurons","Cerebellum, as Purkinje cells","Hypothalamus, as neuroendocrine cells","Spinal cord, as motor neurons"],"answer":1,"why":"SVZ neuroblasts (A cells) travel forward in chains and mature into GABA interneurons of the olfactory bulb (s332-adult)."},
  {"id":"c332-an02","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A researcher identifies a slowly dividing astrocyte in the adult SVZ that forms a glial scaffold and gives rise to rapidly dividing precursors. What is this cell called?","choices":["A cell","Bergmann glia","B cell","Purkinje cell","C cell"],"answer":2,"why":"B cells are slowly dividing astrocytes that give rise to C cells, which become A cells (s332-adult)."},
  {"id":"c332-an03","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"In an adult rodent experiment, one growth factor infused into the ventricles mainly produces glial cells that spread to the striatum, the corpus callosum and the olfactory bulb. Which factor was infused?","choices":["EGF","BDNF","bFGF","IGF-I","NPY"],"answer":0,"why":"Intraventricular EGF mainly promotes gliogenesis in the SVZ, sending cells to the bulb, striatum and callosum; bFGF favors neurons (s332-adult)."},
  {"id":"c332-an04","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A postpartum researcher learns that pregnancy in mice raises SVZ progenitor proliferation and olfactory bulb interneurons. Which hormone is thought responsible?","choices":["Oxytocin","Cortisol","Thyroxine","Estradiol","Prolactin"],"answer":4,"why":"Raised prolactin, by injection or natural pregnancy, boosts mouse SVZ progenitors, possibly aiding learning of infant scents (s332-adult)."},
  {"id":"c332-an05","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"Male rodents repeatedly face territorial intruders. Their glucocorticoid levels rise, and hippocampal neurogenesis falls. Through which local signal is the effect thought to act?","choices":["Serotonin","NPY","Dopamine","Acetylcholine","Glutamate"],"answer":4,"why":"HPA activation in social stress reduces hippocampal neurogenesis apparently through local glutamate signaling (s332-adult)."},
  {"id":"c332-an06","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A 28-year-old woman develops epilepsy. Her resident reads that in a kindling model, newly generated neurons behave abnormally. What did that model show?","choices":["New neurons failed to survive beyond one day","New neurons formed only glia","New neurons migrated to wrong positions and reinforced seizures","New neurons replaced damaged cells and stopped seizures","Neurogenesis ceased entirely after seizures"],"answer":2,"why":"In kindling, new neurons settled incorrectly and joined aberrant circuits, reinforcing the epileptic state (s332-adult)."},
  {"id":"c332-an07","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"Researchers irradiate the hippocampus of mice, reducing neuronal precursors by 85 percent, then give fluoxetine or imipramine. What result does the chapter report?","choices":["Neurogenesis without behavioral recovery","Neither neurogenesis nor behavioral recovery","Normal neurogenesis with behavioral recovery","Recovery only with imipramine","Behavioral recovery without neurogenesis"],"answer":1,"why":"After X-irradiation removed 85 percent of precursors, neither drug induced neurogenesis or behavioral recovery (s332-adult)."},
  {"id":"c332-an08","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A trainee wants a task most sensitive to reduced dentate gyrus neurogenesis in animals. Which task should she choose?","choices":["Pain withdrawal reflex","Simple visual detection","Trace memory linking stimuli separated in time","Motor coordination on a rotarod","Taste preference"],"answer":2,"why":"Reduced dentate neurogenesis impairs formation of trace memories, a hippocampal-dependent task (s332-adult)."},
  {"id":"c332-an09","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A study using hippocampal cultures shows that a peptide from dentate hilar interneurons stimulates precursor proliferation. Through which receptor does it act?","choices":["NPY Y5 receptor","NPY Y1 receptor","Serotonin 1A receptor","NPY Y2 receptor","Glucocorticoid receptor"],"answer":1,"why":"Endogenous NPY stimulates precursor proliferation selectively via Y1, not Y2 or Y5 (s332-adult)."},
  {"id":"c332-an10","tag":"Adult neurogenesis","q":"A 70-year-old man has high cortisol, a small hippocampus on MRI and memory complaints. A resident links this to animal findings. Which finding is most relevant?","choices":["High glucocorticoids suppress dentate neurogenesis in aged rats","EGF drives gliogenesis","bFGF drives olfactory neurons","Songbird neurogenesis rises with song learning","Prolactin boosts SVZ progenitors"],"answer":0,"why":"In aged rats, raised glucocorticoids inhibit dentate neurogenesis, reversible by steroid antagonists and adrenalectomy, which may bear on human cortisol, smaller hippocampi and memory deficits (s332-adult)."}
]);
